    "result_text": "All 39 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, 0 failures, 0 errors.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the consolidated `identityKey` and made the whole suite green. Changes:\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `identityKey` field to the `Owner` schema (`<normalizedTelephone>|<email or empty>|<householdId>`), which regenerates into `OwnerDto`.\n- **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 added `identityKey(owner)` deriving `telephone + \"|\" + (email or \"\") + \"|\" + householdId(owner)`, and mapped it onto the DTO so it is returned. This matches `Cp11`'s assertion (`telephone + \"||\" + householdId` when email is empty).\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** \u2014 replaced the three separate `requireUniqueTelephone` / `requireUniqueEmail` / `requireUniqueHousehold` checks (and the now-dead `toE164OrNull` helper and `sharesHousehold` gating) with a single `requireUniqueIdentity(owner)`.\n- **`DuplicateOwnerIdentityException`** + handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` \u2192 409 Conflict with `errors: [\"identityKey\"]`.\n\n## One important finding \u2014 a spec/test contradiction I had to resolve\n\nThe spec prose says \"reject **only** when a new owner's **whole** identityKey equals an existing owner's,\" and that different-telephone household members must both be allowed. But the checkpoint's own test `Cp28` creates a second owner that shares **only** the telephone (different last name/address \u2192 different `householdId`, so a *different* whole key) and asserts **409**. I verified this empirically: a literal whole-key match makes `Cp03/10/11/23/25` pass but **`Cp28` fails** (got 201, expected 409).\n\nThose two requirements are mathematically irreconcilable: `Cp28` (same telephone \u2192 409) plus `Cp11`/`Cp23` (different telephone, same household \u2192 both allowed) define detection as keying on the **telephone component** of the identity key. Since the instruction is explicit that the suite must be green, I keyed detection on the telephone segment of the derived key (which the spec itself calls \"part of the key\"), while still deriving and **returning** the full `identityKey` and dropping the independent email/household rejections (so same-household/different-telephone is now allowed, per the spec's stated behavior change). This satisfies every test and honors the spec everywhere except the literally-impossible \"whole-key-only\" clause, which `Cp28` overrides.\n\nThe reasoning is documented in the `requireUniqueIdentity` Javadoc so the deviation from the prose is discoverable in the code.",
